gned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124224 1100 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/acdc98dc5a658e7c945dca00c1a1c0a73d87e3dc98db8071a534a4fd7f215c90.png)" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124224 1100 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124224 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{C → B} &= P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/((-4)(-4) - 6 \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{C → B}[x]_{C} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124224 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124225 1100 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124225 1100 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124225 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$. As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S → B} &= P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 \\cdot 10 - 3 \\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{S → B}[x]_{S} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124225 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124302 1100 "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124302 1100 "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/c6c10ed068cfde656d178c98eb4af979563efc82d589f750b8fa92973f362e2a.png)" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124302 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/034a2a4079120d719f9061c339899f9aed9b50a765200291ca15c500ef647a70.png)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/8d07812ea170bcbb56264468f4cfd0fa8b88cf4f68f9b50d35f61e9e33de45c3.png)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 true] [17592186124302 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{C} &= P_{B → C}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535492 false] [17592186124413 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351759 13194139535492 true] [17592186124413 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351766 13194139535492 true] [17592186124413 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351815 13194139535492 true] [17592186124413 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351822 13194139535492 true] [17592186124413 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351857 13194139535492 true] [17592186124414 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351773 13194139535492 true] [17592186124414 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351808 13194139535492 true] [17592186124414 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351829 13194139535492 true] [17592186124414 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351864 13194139535492 true] [17592186124415 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351780 13194139535492 true] [17592186124415 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351836 13194139535492 true] [17592186124415 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351843 13194139535492 true] [17592186124415 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351871 13194139535492 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351787 13194139535492 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351794 13194139535492 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351801 13194139535492 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351850 13194139535492 true] [17592186124498 1020